= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $D=T^*T-TT^*$. This operator is <self-adjoint>, and
$$
\langle Dx,x\rangle=\|Tx\|^2-\|T^*x\|^2.
$$
If condition (iii) holds, this <quadratic form> vanishes. For a real <inner product>, <polarization identity>[polarization] gives
$$
2\langle Dx,y\rangle=\langle D(x+y),x+y\rangle-\langle Dx,x\rangle-\langle Dy,y\rangle=0.
$$
Therefore $D=0$, which is condition (i). This supplies the nontrivial reverse implication needed for the equivalence.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

By the definition of the <adjoint operator>[adjoint],
$$
\langle Tx,Ty\rangle-\langle T^*x,T^*y\rangle
=\langle x,(T^*T-TT^*)y\rangle.
$$
If (i) holds, the expression is zero for all $x,y$, giving (ii). Conversely, if this expression vanishes for every $x$, nondegeneracy of the <inner product> gives $(T^*T-TT^*)y=0$ for every $y$, so (ii) also implies (i).

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Taking $y=x$ in (ii) gives (iii). Together with (iii)$\Rightarrow$(i) from part (i) and (i)$\Rightarrow$(ii) from part (ii), this completes
$$
\boxed{\text{(i)}\iff\text{(ii)}\iff\text{(iii)}.}
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <normal operator>, (a) gives $\|Tx\|=\|T^*x\|$, so one side is zero exactly when the other is. Hence $\boxed{\ker T=\ker T^*}$.

For real $\lambda$, the <adjoint operator>[adjoint] of $T-\lambda I$ is $T^*-\lambda I$. Expanding both products shows that their difference is $T^*T-TT^*=0$. Thus $T-\lambda I$ is normal, and applying the <kernel of a linear map> conclusion to it gives
$$
\boxed{\ker(T-\lambda I)=\ker(T^*-\lambda I)\quad(\lambda\in\mathbb R).}
$$
This proves equality of each real <eigenspace>, hence the same <eigenvalues> and <eigenvectors> on the stated real <vector space>. If neither has real <eigenvalues>, the conclusion is vacuous. Upon complexification one instead pairs $\lambda$ with $\overline\lambda$ for the <adjoint operator>[adjoint]; no claim of the same complex <eigenvalues> is needed here.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In fact the <adjoint operator>[adjoint] invariance follows from the <orthogonal decomposition> and the invariance under $T$, without using the <normal operator> property. For $x\in V_i$ and $y\in V_j$ with $j\ne i$,
$$
\langle T^*x,y\rangle=\langle x,Ty\rangle=0,
$$
since $Ty\in V_j$. Hence $T^*x$ is <orthogonal> to every other summand and therefore lies in $V_i$. Moreover, for $x,y\in V_i$, the <adjoint operator>[adjoint] identity restricted to that subspace shows
$$
\boxed{T_i^*=T^*|_{V_i}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Restricting $T^*T=TT^*$ to $V_i$, using (i), gives $T_i^*T_i=T_iT_i^*$, so the global <normal operator> property implies the <normal operator> property of every restriction. Conversely, if the restrictions are normal and the <adjoint operator>[adjoint] preserves each summand, then for $x=\sum_i x_i$,
$$
(T^*T-TT^*)x=\sum_i(T_i^*T_i-T_iT_i^*)x_i=0.
$$
Thus $\boxed{T\text{ is normal}\iff\text{every }T_i\text{ is normal}}$, with condition (i) automatic under the stated decomposition.

= 3
{parent=2010/Algebra}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Interpret membership as having a representation with denominator <coprime> to $30$, equivalently having this property in lowest terms. Clearly $0,1\in R$. If $a/b,c/d\in R$ with admissible denominators, then
$$
\frac ab-\frac cd=\frac{ad-bc}{bd},\qquad\frac ab\frac cd=\frac{ac}{bd}.
$$
The product $bd$ is still <coprime> to $30$. These are elements of $R$, proving that $\boxed{R\text{ is a subring of }\mathbb Q}$.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take a reduced fraction $a/b\in R$. If $a\ne0$, its only possible inverse in $\mathbb Q$ is $b/a$. Since $\gcd(a,b)=1$, this is in lowest terms up to sign and belongs to $R$ exactly when $\gcd(a,30)=1$. Zero is not invertible. Therefore
$$
\boxed{R^\times=\{a/b:\gcd(a,b)=1,\ \gcd(ab,30)=1\}.}
$$
Equivalently, these are the <rational numbers> whose <valuations> at $2,3,5$ are all zero.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The intersection $J=I\cap\mathbb Z$ is an additive subgroup of $\mathbb Z$, and $nj\in I\cap\mathbb Z$ for $n\in\mathbb Z$, $j\in J$. Thus it is an <ideal> of $\mathbb Z$, necessarily $d\mathbb Z$ for a unique $d\ge0$.

Because $d\in I$, one has $dR\subseteq I$. Conversely, if $a/b\in I$ with $b$ <coprime> to $30$, multiplication by $b\in R$ gives $a\in I\cap\mathbb Z=d\mathbb Z$. Write $a=dk$; then $a/b=d(k/b)\in dR$. Thus $I=dR$.

Every positive <integer> factors as $d=2^\alpha3^\beta5^\gamma u$ with $\gcd(u,30)=1$. The factor $u$ is a <unit>, so it does not change the <ideal>. The complete distinct list is
$$
\boxed{(0),\qquad (2^\alpha3^\beta5^\gamma)_R\quad(\alpha,\beta,\gamma\ge0).}
$$
They are distinct because the intersection of the displayed nonzero <ideal> with $\mathbb Z$ is $2^\alpha3^\beta5^\gamma\mathbb Z$: admissible denominators cannot cancel any of these <prime> powers. The triple $(0,0,0)$ gives $R$ itself.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The zero <ideal> is a <prime ideal> because $R$ is an <integral domain>. Each of $(2),(3),(5)$ is a <prime ideal>: for $p$ among these three, reduction $a/b\mapsto\overline a\,\overline b^{-1}$ is a surjective <ring homomorphism> $R\to\mathbb F_p$ with <kernel of a ring homomorphism> $(p)$, so its <quotient ring> is a <field>.

Any other nonzero proper <ideal> in (c) has generator $d=2^\alpha3^\beta5^\gamma$ with total exponent at least two. Choose one <prime factor> $p$ and put $e=d/p$. Then $pe=d\in(d)$ but neither $p$ nor $e$ belongs to $(d)$, as seen from the required <prime> exponents. Such an <ideal> is not <prime>. Thus with the standard proper-<ideal> convention the answer is
$$
\boxed{(0),(2),(3),(5).}
$$
**The definition printed in this paper omits properness.** Literally under that definition $R$ itself also satisfies the implication, so its literal complete list is the preceding four <ideals> together with $\boxed{R}$. Both interpretations are thereby accounted for.

= Analysis
